    content:
      "# SOP-07 \u2014 Base Perimeter Defense\n\n_Simulated training document. Not real doctrine._\n\n## 1. Sensor coverage\n\nThe base perimeter is divided into numbered arcs, each covered by at least two sensor types (visual, motion, or patrol observation). A single-sensor arc is a reportable coverage gap and appears on the readiness dashboard.\n\n## 2. Alert confirmation\n\nA single sensor trigger raises severity **low** and tasks the nearest camera or patrol for confirmation. Two independent sensors within 5 sim-minutes and 200 m raise **high** and constitute a confirmed perimeter event \u2014 QRF criteria under SOP-02 apply.\n\n## 3. Response postures\n\n- **Green:** routine; standard patrol rotation.\n- **Amber:** confirmed activity near perimeter; patrols shift to affected arcs; gates restrict to essential movement.\n- **Red:** confirmed breach attempt; QRF deploys; all non-essential external activity halts; commander notified.\n\nPosture changes are operator decisions, logged with reason. The system recommends; it never changes posture autonomously.\n\n## 4. Breach handling\n\nOn a confirmed breach: contain and observe, establish inner cordon, and account for all personnel via the roster. Pursuit beyond the perimeter requires commander authorization. Full sensor and decision timelines feed the after-action report.",
