    content:
      "# SOP-08 \u2014 HADR Deployment\n\n_Simulated training document. Not real doctrine._\n\nHADR is a military mission class: the same units, logistics chains, and command procedures as other operations, applied to disaster response (see scenario SC-8).\n\n## 1. Activation\n\nHADR deployment activates on tasking from the exercise authority. Initial actions: designate the operational area, establish the supply chain, and assign sector responsibilities within 60 sim-minutes of activation.\n\n## 2. Priorities\n\nFixed order unless the commander overrides with logged reason: (1) life-saving movement and medical response (SOP-03 applies unchanged), (2) route clearance for relief movement, (3) supply distribution, (4) infrastructure assessment reporting.\n\n## 3. Logistics tempo\n\nHADR consumes supplies faster than sustainment operations: rations and medical stock are re-forecast every 2 sim-hours, and the resupply threshold rises from 30% to 50% stock. Convoy movements follow SOP-04 with weather as the dominant route cost.\n\n## 4. Coordination and reporting\n\nCivilian-agency liaison is represented as report events only \u2014 no civilian units are simulated under command. Situation reports follow the standard cadence at doubled frequency during the first 12 sim-hours. All KPIs (response time, supply coverage, medical timelines) feed the after-action report and the Ch. 14 benchmark comparisons.",
